mixin TextBoxComponent-Abstract

  p.Article-Paragraph.
    Along with the button component (#[+ImportantEntity--YDID Button]), the text box is one of the most basic and
      popular components of a graphical user interface.
    We are talking about entering text #[+Keyphrase--YDID without formatting] (such as bold or italics), and a
      text editor with support for text formatting is a separate and rather complex topic.
    But despite the lack of formatting, there is a lot of data that is accepted from the user through this
      component: names, addresses, all sorts of titles.
    Moreover, "text" means not&nbsp;only a set of completed sentences, but also any information
      that can be represented as a sequence of characters:

  ul.Article-UnorderedList
    li Email addresses
    li Passwords
    li Links

  p.Article-Paragraph.
    In addition, this component is sometimes used for entering numeric data, although often these are not&nbsp;numbers,
      but sequences of digits sometimes mixed with auxiliary characters:

  ul.Article-UnorderedList
    li Phone numbers
    li Postal codes
    li Credit card numbers

  p.Article-Paragraph.
    In most cases, this component does not&nbsp;provide for line breaks, and when a large number of characters is entered,
      only a part of them is displayed, although scrolling is possible using the arrow keys.
    However, in cases where the user is required to enter exactly completed sentences (maybe even paragraphs),
      the possibility of line breaks is required, which can sometimes be considered as simple text formatting.
    From the point of view of #[+ImportantEntity--YDID HTML], a separate type of element is used for multi-line text (and
      automatic height adjustment is not&nbsp;initially provided), but in #[+ImportantEntity--YDID YDF] to
      support multi-line mode, you only need to specify the corresponding option, and automatic height adjustment
      is also available.

  p.Article-Paragraph
    | As in the case of buttons, the simplicity of the text field is #[+Keyword--YDID deceptive].
    | Firstly, the text input field is a
    |
    +Link--YDF({
      internalURI: localizedRouting.coreLibrary.$children.GUI_Components.$children.controls.$children.validatables.$URI,
    }) validatable component
    | , and therefore all the complexity of the task of #[+Term--YDID validation] of input data has a #[+Keyword--YDID direct]
    |   relation to it.
    | In #[+ImportantEntity--YDID YDF], a text input field, like other components wrapped in the
    |
    +Link--YDF({
      internalURI: localizedRouting.coreLibrary.$children.GUI_Components.$children.controls.$children.
          ValidatableControlShell.$URI,
    }) ValidatableControlShell
    |   component, includes optional auxiliary elements such as a label, a comment, a list of validation errors, and
    |   so on, although from the point of view of #[+ImportantEntity--YDID HTML] these are all separate elements.

  p.Article-Paragraph.
    The text input field has many states.
    #[+Keyphrase--YDID Visually distinguishable] should be #[+Keyphrase--YDID at least]:

  dl.Article-DescriptionList

    dt: +ImportantEntity__Code--YDID :hover
    dd.
      Activated when the mouse cursor is hovered over, letting the user know that the system is responding to their actions and if
        they click on the input field, they will be able to start typing.

    dt: +ImportantEntity__Code--YDID :focus
    dd.
      Activated when the text field has taken focus, that is, keyboard input will be accepted inside this
        text field.

    dt: +ImportantEntity__Code--YDID :readonly/:disabled
    dd.
      Active when the input field is read-only.
      This functionality is not&nbsp;as rare as it seems, because many applications have a
        view mode and an edit mode.

    dt Invalid state
    dd.
      Active when the entered value is not valid, and also if the validity display mode
        is active.
      If the input field is initially empty and mandatory, the empty value is #[+Keyword--YDID invalid]
        #[+Keyword__Contrast--YDID from the beginning], however, before highlighting the field in red and informing the
        user who has not&nbsp;yet done anything about validation errors, the user should be allowed to fill in this field, and only
        when they remove focus from the input field can the validity display mode be activated.
      Note that such a concept #[+Keyphrase--YDID from the HTML/CSS point of view]
        #[+Keyword--YDID does not exist], and therefore if validation is defined through #[+Term--YDID HTML attributes] like
        #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID required] and #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID minlength] and styles for the
        #[+Term--YDID pseudoclass] #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID :invalid], then these styles will be applied regardless
        of whether the user has started entering data into empty fields or not yet.

  p.Article-Paragraph.
    Moreover, #[+Keyword--YDID some] of these states can occur #[+Keyword--YDID simultaneously].
    For example, the #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID hover] state can occur #[+Keyword--YDID simultaneously] with
      #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID focus], and #[+Keyword--YDID both] of these states can occur on top of
      #[+ImportantEntity__Code--YDID invalid].
    This raises the question of style priority.

  p.Article-Paragraph.
    Also, in practice, the following additional functionality is in demand for the input field:

  ul.Article-UnorderedList

    li Icons, which, depending on the design, can be either on the left or on the right
    li Password show/hide button
    li Button for copying the entered value

  p.Article-Paragraph.
    All this functionality is available in the #[+ImportantEntity--YDID TextBox] component.


